shortAnswer: "A system prompt is a standing instruction that applies to every conversation, rather than being typed each time. It's where you put the things that are always true — who the AI is helping, what tone to use, what it must never do, what format to return. It's the single highest-leverage change most teams can make, because it fixes the same problem for everyone at once."
faq:
  - q: "Where do I put a system prompt?"
    a: "It depends on the product. Most business AI tools expose it as 'custom instructions', 'project instructions' or a settings field. In team plans an administrator can often set one for everybody, which is where the real leverage is."
  - q: "How long should it be?"
    a: "Long enough to cover what's genuinely always true, and no longer. A focused half-page beats three pages of everything anyone could think of — the model has to weigh all of it against every request, and padding dilutes the parts that matter."
  - q: "Does a system prompt use up the context window?"
    a: "Yes — it's included in every request. That's another argument for keeping it tight. A bloated system prompt costs you room on every single conversation."
  - q: "Can it stop the AI making things up?"
    a: "It helps, and it's worth including an explicit instruction to say when it doesn't know rather than guess. It reduces the problem rather than removing it, so the review step still matters."
  - q: "Should each team have its own?"
    a: "Usually yes. Finance and marketing want different defaults. Shared company-wide rules (what's off limits, what tone) plus a team-specific layer works better than one prompt trying to serve everyone."
