<p>
  Most teams using AI have the same experience: it's genuinely useful, but they
  find themselves typing the same preamble every time. "Write in Australian
  English." "We're a B2B business." "Don't use exclamation marks." "Keep it
  under 200 words."
</p>

<p>
  A system prompt is where those instructions go so you stop repeating them.
</p>

<h2>How it differs from what you type</h2>

<p>
  Your prompt is the request. The system prompt is the standing brief that sits
  behind every request — read by the model before it sees your message at all.
</p>

<p>
  It's the difference between telling a new starter the house rules on their
  first day and re-explaining them at the start of every task. The rules haven't
  changed; you've just stopped paying the cost of repeating them.
</p>

<h2>What belongs in one</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Who it's helping and at what.</strong> "You are assisting the operations team at an Australian offshore staffing business." Context shapes every answer that follows.</li>
  <li><strong>Tone and language.</strong> Australian English, plain language, no marketing filler. Be specific — "professional" means nothing.</li>
  <li><strong>Hard rules.</strong> Never invent figures. Never give legal or financial advice. Always flag uncertainty rather than guessing.</li>
  <li><strong>Default format.</strong> If most outputs should be short paragraphs rather than bullet lists, say so once here.</li>
  <li><strong>Words to avoid.</strong> Jargon you've banned, competitor names, phrasing that clashes with your brand.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What doesn't belong</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Facts that change.</strong> Prices, staff names, current promotions. These go stale silently and the model states them with total confidence.</li>
  <li><strong>Task-specific detail.</strong> If it only applies to one job, it belongs in that request.</li>
  <li><strong>Long documents.</strong> Pasting a whole brand guide costs context on every conversation. Summarise the rules that actually bite.</li>
</ul>

<h2>An Australian small-business example</h2>

<p>
  A Perth building supplies company has four staff using AI for customer emails.
  Each writes differently, so the outputs vary — some too casual, one prone to
  American spelling, another producing walls of text.
</p>

<p>
  They write a shared system prompt: Australian English, short paragraphs, no
  exclamation marks, always offer a phone call as the next step, never quote a
  price without checking, sign off with the team name.
</p>

<p>
  The variation collapses immediately. Not because anyone got better at
  prompting, but because the standing rules stopped depending on whether each
  person remembered them at 4pm.
</p>

<h2>Writing a good one</h2>

<p>
  Start from output you didn't like. Every time the AI produces something
  slightly wrong — too formal, wrong spelling, too long — that's a candidate
  rule. Add it once and the correction becomes permanent.
</p>

<p>
  Be concrete. "Be professional" gives the model nothing. "Write like you're
  emailing a busy tradesperson: short sentences, no jargon, get to the point in
  the first line" gives it something to act on.
</p>

<p>
  Then review it every few months. System prompts accumulate rules written for
  problems that no longer occur, and each one costs a little clarity on
  everything else.
</p>

<h2>Why this is the highest-leverage change</h2>

<p>
  Better prompting is a skill each person has to learn individually. A system
  prompt is a fix written once that raises the floor for everybody — including
  the people who will never get particularly good at prompting and shouldn't
  have to.
</p>

<p>
  If a team is getting inconsistent AI output, this is almost always the first
  thing to fix, and almost always cheaper than whatever else is being considered.
</p>
